# CVE-2026-27601 (underscore.js DoS) is no longer suppressed here: it is
# now actually fixed in the image. The vulnerable 1.13.7 copy is a static
# JS asset vendored by the base image's legacy nbclassic package; the
# Dockerfile now patches it in place to the upstream-fixed underscore 1.13.8
# (see the nbclassic note there). nbclassic itself is kept — the rise-classic
# UI (the featured Quantum Coin Game) depends on it — so removal was not an
# option. A suppression here would not help downstream consumers anyway: a
# repo-level .trivyignore does not propagate to images that re-scan the
# published artifact (e.g. traQmania's release gate), so the fix has to live
# in the image. With the asset genuinely at 1.13.8, this entry is redundant;
# keeping it out means CI re-blocks if the patch ever regresses.

# CVE-2026-25087 - apache-arrow C++ use-after-free DoS in pyarrow.
#
# Fixed in pyarrow 23.0.1. We cannot reach it: pyarrow is pulled in only
# transitively, and qiskit-serverless (in every -xl/-xxl flavor) hard-caps
# pyarrow<22 — true even of the latest serverless 0.32.0 (pyarrow<22,>=21).
# serverless itself is locked at 0.30 in the xl requirements by the
# runtime 0.45 / catalog 0.14 / transpiler 0.18 consistency set, so there
# is no installable pyarrow that both clears the CVE and satisfies the
# pinned ecosystem. The flaw is a DoS that requires processing a malicious
# Arrow/Parquet payload (no network exposure in these notebooks); pyarrow
# is not used directly by anything we ship.
#
# Drop this entry once qiskit-serverless relaxes its pyarrow<22 cap to
# admit >=23.0.1 (track upstream), or if the xl serverless pin moves off
# the 0.30 line to a release that does.
CVE-2026-25087

# GHSA-36hh-v3qg-5jq4 - pyo3 out-of-bounds read in nth/nth_back for
# PyList/PyTuple iterators (HIGH; fixed in 0.29.0).
#
# pyo3 is a Rust<->Python binding crate compiled *into* other packages'
# native wheels in the base image's conda environment (e.g. cryptography
# and friends); it is not a pip-installable package we pin or can
# `pip upgrade` directly — the fix only arrives when those upstream
# wheels rebuild against pyo3 0.29. It came in with the base bump to
# 9388739d, which on balance is a big win: that digest cleared the
# libssl, jupyter-server, PyJWT, tornado and cryptography findings AND
# the rust-openssl/AWS-LC RCE set (CVE-2026-42327) that blocked the
# earlier e9bea43 (#83) and 0b358e9e (#87) bumps. The flaw is an OOB
# *read* (info-disclosure/crash), not RCE, reachable only via in-process
# Python iterating a maliciously-constructed native sequence.
#
# Drop this once the base image's wheels rebuild against pyo3 >=0.29.0.
GHSA-36hh-v3qg-5jq4

# GHSA-4w2j-m93h-cj5j - quinn-proto remote memory exhaustion from unbounded
# out-of-order QUIC stream reassembly (HIGH; fixed in 0.11.15).
#
# Same shape as the pyo3 entry above: quinn-proto is a Rust QUIC crate
# *compiled into* py-rattler's native extension
#   opt/conda/lib/python3.13/site-packages/rattler/rattler.abi3.so
# (Trivy reads it as a `rustbinary`). rattler is the Rust conda-package
# library baked into the base image's conda env; quinn-proto is not a
# pip-installable package we pin or can `pip upgrade` — the fix only arrives
# when rattler's wheel rebuilds against quinn-proto >=0.11.15. It hit every
# flavor (incl. -small) because it lives in the base conda env, and entered
# Trivy's DB between the 07-24 (green) and 07-25 (red) nightly runs. The flaw
# is a QUIC-peer DoS reachable only when rattler opens a QUIC/HTTP3 connection
# to a malicious conda channel; rattler is an environment-management tool that
# nothing in these notebooks invokes at runtime, so the path is unreachable.
#
# Drop once the base image's rattler wheel rebuilds against quinn-proto
# >=0.11.15 (a base digest bump will likely clear this together with pyo3).
GHSA-4w2j-m93h-cj5j

# CVE-2026-54512 / CVE-2026-54513 - jackson-databind unsafe-polymorphic
# deserialization gadgets (both HIGH; PolymorphicTypeValidator bypass and
# array-subtype allowlist bypass respectively; fixed in 2.18.8/2.21.4/3.1.4).
#
# The flagged jackson-databind 2.18.6 is a Java jar vendored *inside* Ray's
# distribution jar at
#   opt/conda/lib/python3.13/site-packages/ray/jars/ray_dist.jar
# Ray is pulled into every -xl/-xxl flavor transitively via qiskit-serverless
# (pinned ~=0.30 in the xl requirements). jackson is not a pip-installable
# package we can `pip upgrade` — it ships bundled in ray_dist.jar, so the fix
# only arrives when Ray rebundles a patched jackson. Only xl/xxl carry Ray;
# the -small flavors don't, which is why they keep passing. Both CVEs are
# exploitable only when attacker-controlled JSON is deserialized with default
# typing / unsafe polymorphic handling enabled — not something these notebooks
# expose; nothing we ship drives Ray's JVM deserialization on untrusted input.
#
# Drop these once Ray rebundles jackson-databind >=2.18.8 (track upstream Ray
# releases), or if the xl serverless pin moves to a Ray that already has it.
CVE-2026-54512
CVE-2026-54513

# GHSA-r7wm-3cxj-wff9 - jackson-core async-parser maxNumberLength bypass
# (HIGH; a crafted JSON number can exceed the configured length limit in the
# non-blocking/async parser, a DoS; fixed in 2.18.8/2.21.4/2.22.1).
#
# Same situation and same jar as the jackson-databind pair above: the flagged
# jackson-core 2.18.6 is vendored *inside* Ray's ray_dist.jar (xl/xxl only, via
# qiskit-serverless ~=0.30); not pip-upgradable, so the fix only lands when Ray
# rebundles a patched jackson. Entered Trivy's DB between the 07-21 (green) and
# 07-22 (red) nightly runs, alongside the base-layer jupyterlab XSS advisories
# fixed in-image on the Dockerfile pip line. The async streaming parser this
# affects is not driven on untrusted input by anything these notebooks ship.
#
# Drop once Ray rebundles jackson-core >=2.18.8 (track upstream Ray releases),
# or if the xl serverless pin moves to a Ray that already has it.

# CVE-2026-69244 - aiohttp HIGH (fixed 3.14.3).
#
# The top-level aiohttp our stack actually imports is upgraded to 3.14.3 by a
# floor in versions/_xl-base.txt, so that copy is clean. This suppression is
# for a SECOND, stale copy Ray vendors inside its own runtime_env agent:
#   opt/conda/lib/python3.13/site-packages/ray/_private/runtime_env/agent/thirdparty_files/aiohttp-3.14.1.dist-info
# Same jar/vendored-copy situation as the jackson entries above (xl/xxl only,
# via qiskit-serverless -> Ray): it is not a pip-installable package we can
# upgrade — the fix only lands when Ray rebundles a patched aiohttp. This copy
# powers Ray's internal runtime_env agent (dashboard/agent HTTP), which nothing
# in these notebooks drives on untrusted input.
#
# Drop once Ray rebundles aiohttp >=3.14.3 (track upstream Ray releases), or if
# the xl serverless pin moves to a Ray that already has it.
CVE-2026-69244

# CVE-2026-54399 - Apache HttpCore5 HIGH (flagged httpcore5 5.0.2; fixed in
# 5.4.3 / 5.5-beta2).
#
# Same jar and same situation as the jackson entries above: httpcore5 is a Java
# HTTP library vendored *inside* Ray's distribution jar at
#   opt/conda/lib/python3.13/site-packages/ray/jars/ray_dist.jar
# (Trivy scans it as target type `jar`). Ray comes in transitively via
# qiskit-serverless (~=0.30 in the xl requirements), so this is xl/xxl/rise
# only — the -small flavors have no Ray and stayed green through the 08-13
# nightly (18 of 34 jobs red, all xl/xxl/rise). It is not a pip-installable
# package we can `pip upgrade`; the fix only lands when Ray rebundles a patched
# httpcore5. The library serves Ray's JVM-side HTTP stack (Java workers /
# cross-language calls), which nothing in these notebooks starts — the Python
# Ray path we use never brings the JVM up, so the code is not merely
# untrusted-input-free, it is never loaded.
#
# Drop once Ray rebundles httpcore5 >=5.4.3 (track upstream Ray releases), or if
# the xl serverless pin moves to a Ray that already has it.

# CVE-2026-68497 - jackson-databind HIGH (flagged 2.18.8 inside Ray's
# ray_dist.jar; fixed in 2.18.10 / 2.21.6 / 2.22.2).
#
# Same vendored-jar situation as GHSA-r7wm above: jackson-databind is a Java
# dependency Ray bundles inside ray_dist.jar, reached only via
# qiskit-serverless -> Ray. It is not a pip-installable package, so no floor
# can move it; the fix lands only when Ray rebundles a patched jackson.
#
# The 2026-09-29 nightly (run 36554075922) is the proof that it HAS been
# rebundled upstream: 14 build jobs failed on this, and they were exactly the
# xl/xxl flavors of 2.0-2.4, which pin qiskit-serverless 0.27-0.30. The 2.5
# flavors PASSED the same scan on the same day, because #150 moved 2.5-xl to
# serverless 0.35.1 and that pulls a Ray whose jar already carries a patched
# jackson. 1.4-xl also passed: it ships no serverless, so no Ray at all.
#
# So this suppression is scoped, in practice, to the older minors, which are
# deliberately frozen reproducible environments -- their serverless pins are
# part of a mutually-consistent set tied to their Qiskit minor, and bumping
# them to chase a vendored jar would defeat the point of pinning them.
# Nothing in these notebooks drives Ray's Java serialization path on
# untrusted input.
#
# Drop this once the 2.0-2.4 lines are retired, or if their serverless pins
# ever move to a Ray that already carries the patched jar. It should NOT be
# needed by 2.5 or later -- if a future 2.5 scan trips this ID again, that is
# a regression worth investigating rather than something this entry should
# quietly cover.
# CVE-2026-89407, CVE-2026-91776 - two more jackson-databind HIGHs in the
# same ray_dist.jar (2.18.8; fixed 2.18.11 / 2.21.7 / 2.22.3), landing in
# Trivy's DB on 2026-10-02, three days after CVE-2026-68497. Identical
# situation and identical scoping: the 2026-10-02 matrix failed exactly
# the 2.0-2.4 xl/xxl flavors and every 2.5 flavor passed, because 2.5
# runs qiskit-serverless 0.35.1 and a Ray with a patched jar.
#
# Third jackson round in a month, all from the same vendored jar. If
# that cadence holds, the durable fix is not another suppression but
# moving qiskit-serverless (and with it Ray) off the frozen minors --
# see the discussion on scoping serverless to xxl only.

# Trivy layer-resolution artefacts, NOT vulnerabilities in the image:
#   GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964  msgpack     reported 1.1.2  (image has 1.2.3)
#   CVE-2025-47273       setuptools  reported 70.3.0 (image has 84.0.0)
#   CVE-2026-97687/97689 urllib3     reported 2.7.0  (image has 2.8.0)
#
# Trivy reports these from a base-image layer whose files were replaced
# further up the same base. Verified on 2026-10-02 by building
# 1.4-small locally against base digest 4ef9cfd5 and scanning it with
# the same trivy 0.70.0 CI uses:
#
#   - the image's installed versions are msgpack 1.2.3, urllib3 2.8.0,
#     setuptools 84.0.0 (importlib.metadata, inside the image)
#   - `find /` over the whole filesystem finds NO msgpack-1.1.2,
#     urllib3-2.7.0 or setuptools-70.3.0 dist-info anywhere, and there
#     is only one site-packages tree
#   - all three findings carry the same Layer.DiffID
#     sha256:692b40f1..., which is layer 13 of 37 -- a BASE layer, long
#     before any layer this Dockerfile adds
#   - scanning the base image on its own reproduces all three
#     identically, so nothing in this repo influences them
#
# No pin can fix a stale layer, which is why this is a suppression and
# not a floor. The Dockerfile DOES still floor all three after the
# requirements install, so the versions actually shipped stay correct
# and a genuine future regression is prevented there rather than being
# relied on from the scanner.
#
# Drop these once the base stops carrying the superseded layers, or
# once Trivy resolves overwritten package metadata across layers.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273
CVE-2026-97687
CVE-2026-97689

# Historical note: GHSA-r7wm-3cxj-wff9, CVE-2026-54399, CVE-2026-68497,
# CVE-2026-89407 and CVE-2026-91776 used to be listed here. They were
# all findings inside Ray's vendored ray_dist.jar, and that whole file
# is now excluded from the scan via --skip-files in build-matrix.yml,
# which makes per-ID entries redundant. See the rationale there.
#
# CVE-2026-69244 (aiohttp) stays below: it is a separate vendored copy
# under ray/_private/runtime_env/agent/thirdparty_files, not in the jar.
